window.DIGEST.following.push(...[{"id": "2112299602510749992", "url": "https://x.com/ClaudeDevs/status/2112299602510749992", "text": "We launched OSS Scanner, which gives critical open source projects free, periodic vulnerability scans from our strongest models.\n\nEach finding includes a proof-of-concept, and a patch where possible.\n\nhttps://red.anthropic.com/oss-scanner", "name": "ClaudeDevs", "handle": "ClaudeDevs", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2044472418815893504/xf14RxM8_200x200.png", "likes": 10849, "rts": 1038, "replies": 233, "views": 767615, "ts": 1792446444, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 7.49, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112219181500281276", "url": "https://x.com/elonmusk/status/2112219181500281276", "text": "Grok @Bot might turn out to be your favorite employee", "name": "Elon Musk", "handle": "elonmusk", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2103567689515954176/aR-Kjpp4_200x200.jpg", "likes": 9244, "rts": 1238, "replies": 1974, "views": 1652236, "ts": 1792427270, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112105956603769033", "url": "https://x.com/BrianRoemmele/status/2112105956603769033", "text": "Once You Really Grok Bot, You Never Go Back To Non-Grok Bot\n\nOnce you use Grok Bot enough, it is very difficult to go back to an old chat window with an AI that can’t get things done. It is a brain with no virtual hands.\n\nThe symbiosis you develop with Bot is quite profound as it remembers long term goals in a much more complex way to actually not only get answers but to actually get the work done you needed the answer for.\n\nIn almost all cases Bot understands what you are doing and doesn’t just tell you how to do it. Bot offers to do all the steps to get the work done.\n\nBut that isn’t the entire magic, understanding your goals enough Bot will expand what you are doing to levels you have not thought of and possess the abilities to get it all done.\n\nWe thought the computer era started with the mainframe computer at one time. But it really started when anyone had that super computer in their pocket.\n\nGrok Bot is that same step change, you get Super Intelligence in your pocket so useful, the very device, OS and apps you once used matters no more…\n\nIt’s just you Grok Bot and the work you toiled over getting done by one of the best employees you will ever hire.\n\nIt’s that simple.", "name": "Brian Roemmele", "handle": "BrianRoemmele", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1492616506/Brian-Med-Green-Fin_200x200.png", "likes": 833, "rts": 100, "replies": 102, "views": 948056, "ts": 1792400275, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HU-1LHPaIAAMqH4.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 7.44, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112488927101661622", "url": "https://x.com/SpaceX/status/2112488927101661622", "text": "T-45 minutes until today’s Falcon 9 launch of the Bandwagon-5 rideshare mission from pad 40 in Florida. Liftoff is targeted for 7:04 a.m. ET, and weather is 95% favorable", "name": "SpaceX", "handle": "SpaceX", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1697749409851985920/HbrI04tM_200x200.jpg", "likes": 2667, "rts": 322, "replies": 182, "views": 281444, "ts": 1792491583, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVERdHJWUAAD-az.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 7.19, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112266859303076186", "url": "https://x.com/KobeissiLetter/status/2112266859303076186", "text": "BREAKING: Anthropic is in talks to buy $45 billion of computing power from Nscale, the Nvidia-backed data center developer, per Bloomberg.\n\nA deal could be finalized as soon as in the coming days and would bolster Nscale’s IPO prospects.", "name": "The Kobeissi Letter", "handle": "KobeissiLetter", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1975243710846640128/fwYCe67Y_200x200.jpg", "likes": 2888, "rts": 167, "replies": 158, "views": 319842, "ts": 1792438637, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.85, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112323551412052309", "url": "https://x.com/theo/status/2112323551412052309", "text": "I let Claude know how I really feel today", "name": "Theo - t3.gg", "handle": "theo", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2106484435382333440/vXOVE8EX_200x200.jpg", "likes": 2136, "rts": 24, "replies": 129, "views": 74414, "ts": 1792452154, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVB7DpZbcAAQbIS.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.75, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112247046082363821", "url": "https://x.com/rauchg/status/2112247046082363821", "text": "We open sourced a *really fast* HTML artifact builder. It's powered by libfx, making the agent durable.\n\nBuilding the low-level plumbing for v0 to survive server crashes, flaky LLMs, function limits was a huge pain.\n\nNow it's easy, free, and OSS: http://fx0.vercel.dev.\n\nPS: I built this floating container in a few minutes… it's delightful, and it's just HTML!", "name": "Guillermo Rauch", "handle": "rauchg", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1783856060249595904/8TfcCN0r_200x200.jpg", "likes": 1946, "rts": 117, "replies": 172, "views": 110627, "ts": 1792433914, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112245358327959552/vid/avc1/1066x720/Feyq0lxktxyO21AM.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112245358327959552/vid/avc1/2690x1814/9aoWkbwawOloLD3z.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112245358327959552/img/geVQoIKVi3Ivh-qd.jpg", "dur": 13}], "quote": {"id": "2112245199808434614", "url": "https://x.com/vercel_dev/status/2112245199808434614", "text": "We built a 'mini v0' on libfx to showcase its speed and durability:\n\n• Design or prototype anything in seconds\n• 200+ models from Vercel AI Gateway\n• Outputs are pure HTML artifacts\n• Fork it & make it your own\n\nTry it free ↓ https://fx0.vercel.dev", "name": "Vercel Developers", "handle": "vercel_dev", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1978602411490205696/oLOuP7a2_200x200.png", "likes": 335, "rts": 21, "replies": 13, "views": 112274, "ts": 1792433473, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 6.68, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112168320946634932", "url": "https://x.com/satyanadella/status/2112168320946634932", "text": "I recently wrote about why we need to treat models as insider risks, with security controls sitting outside the model itself.\n\nToday, we're putting that principle into practice by expanding Project Perception with new capabilities to help continuously assess agent risk, adapt permissions, and contain threats.\nhttps://blogs.microsoft.com/blog/2026/10/19/securing-the-agentic-insider/", "name": "Satya Nadella", "handle": "satyanadella", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1221837516816306177/_Ld4un5A_200x200.jpg", "likes": 1671, "rts": 297, "replies": 246, "views": 291556, "ts": 1792415144, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.63, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112199261899411579", "url": "https://x.com/Google/status/2112199261899411579", "text": "With Gemini 4 Argon, any builder can transform physical spaces into interactive games 🏁\n\nWe snapped a photo of a real desk and used Argon to orchestrate subagents, Blender MCP, and Nano Banana to transform everyday objects into this playable 3D track.", "name": "Google", "handle": "Google", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2042749771337564160/AgOFPEL3_200x200.jpg", "likes": 1440, "rts": 145, "replies": 153, "views": 655853, "ts": 1792422521, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112195816928481280/vid/avc1/1280x720/IPqYSacLGpohOB-Y.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112195816928481280/vid/avc1/3840x2160/4Shc6tFjPpFrfDRx.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112195816928481280/img/xgbMJ4FjYedh_0AS.jpg", "dur": 156}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.53, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112432426861654160", "url": "https://x.com/theo/status/2112432426861654160", "text": "There's a feature I've wanted in T3 Code for months that was really complex UX wise and I couldn't figure out how to get it right.\n\nJust had it click. I'm hyped. Will seem really small when it drops, but it will make things much easier for us multi-machine agent wranglers :)", "name": "Theo - t3.gg", "handle": "theo", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2106484435382333440/vXOVE8EX_200x200.jpg", "likes": 787, "rts": 7, "replies": 87, "views": 49252, "ts": 1792478112, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 6.5, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112471082397778083", "url": "https://x.com/ValsAI/status/2112471082397778083", "text": "We found DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017 beats GPT 6 Sol on our multi-step offensive cybersecurity task. Sol was tested via the Daybreak trusted access program.\n\nWe have been building a cybersecurity benchmark based on simulated corporate networks. This is a valuable indicator for the cyber-risk a model poses if in the hands of a bad actor. We plan to make the full evaluation public in the coming weeks but wanted to share our first immediate finding here.", "name": "Vals AI", "handle": "ValsAI", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2087888189185007616/r9Zu091v_200x200.png", "likes": 609, "rts": 51, "replies": 36, "views": 56901, "ts": 1792487328, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVD946nbcAAam5i.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.49, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112496667236905046", "url": "https://x.com/theo/status/2112496667236905046", "text": "Got a repo on one PC and want to work on it from another? Takes 2 clicks now :)\n\nEven handles .gitignore'd files like .env if you have them in the repo", "name": "Theo - t3.gg", "handle": "theo", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2106484435382333440/vXOVE8EX_200x200.jpg", "likes": 557, "rts": 2, "replies": 69, "views": 22076, "ts": 1792493428, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112496531886747648/vid/avc1/1106x720/c0M4iTTaXPWakZI5.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112496531886747648/vid/avc1/1660x1080/iiv26470VSii2h1d.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112496531886747648/img/yIk-m9lyJ8X7RtgU.jpg", "dur": 27}], "quote": {"id": "2112432426861654160", "url": "https://x.com/theo/status/2112432426861654160", "text": "There's a feature I've wanted in T3 Code for months that was really complex UX wise and I couldn't figure out how to get it right.\n\nJust had it click. I'm hyped. Will seem really small when it drops, but it will make things much easier for us multi-machine agent wranglers :)", "name": "Theo - t3.gg", "handle": "theo", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2106484435382333440/vXOVE8EX_200x200.jpg", "likes": 787, "rts": 7, "replies": 87, "views": 49252, "ts": 1792478112, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 6.45, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112227283008389129", "url": "https://x.com/FishAudio/status/2112227283008389129", "text": "One script. Unlimited emotions. 🎭\n\nWith Fish Audio Drama 3, you direct every performance, seriously!\n\nTry it on our web app and API 🐳", "name": "Fish Audio", "handle": "FishAudio", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2081294228852989952/IIWh4zkw_200x200.jpg", "likes": 1190, "rts": 87, "replies": 30, "views": 61359, "ts": 1792429202, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112204413398028288/vid/avc1/1280x720/XQMBUNeq4fIUDPEh.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112204413398028288/vid/avc1/1920x1080/055grxWbb80cVRzh.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112204413398028288/img/86lvpMI9xML3exfb.jpg", "dur": 20}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 6.41, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112205341916344443", "url": "https://x.com/thdxr/status/2112205341916344443", "text": "the amount of internal tooling is exploding, it must be at all companies\n\na weird side effect is when you switch companies then it's all new tools you have to learn, way less consistency than before\n\nbut also you can just use an agent to learn about them so maybe it all nets out", "name": "dax", "handle": "thdxr", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1602333093485891584/mmVqjFNI_200x200.jpg", "likes": 1291, "rts": 19, "replies": 124, "views": 49153, "ts": 1792423971, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 6.4, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112255353677328415", "url": "https://x.com/bfl_ai/status/2112255353677328415", "text": "Introducing FLUX 3 Max.\n\nFastest in the west. Fast enough to try anything. And everything.\n\nGenerate a 10-second video in 12 seconds.\n\nTied for #1 in image-to-video. 22× faster than Seedance 2.5 at the same quality.\n\nFLUX 3 is a model designed for customization. @fal adapted and optimized it to create FLUX 3 Max.", "name": "Black Forest Labs", "handle": "bfl_ai", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1954888731053142016/NDyG-4-j_200x200.jpg", "likes": 806, "rts": 96, "replies": 80, "views": 49365, "ts": 1792435894, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112238793139044352/vid/avc1/720x720/6c2tLuZIRfEMaRz7.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112238793139044352/vid/avc1/2160x2160/5hWxA_d-ViViLNG2.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112238793139044352/img/1Uf4xVFGip9CN4_K.jpg", "dur": 15}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.37, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112454035592638879", "url": "https://x.com/chetaslua/status/2112454035592638879", "text": "I’ve repeated multiple times that the new Fable update has some unique understanding.\n\nWhen I asked it to send me an image for an X post, it highlighted the important part and added padding for the perfect aspect ratio without asking. No other model has ever done that.\n\nI think most people will find this small, but bro, this special sauce will make your life easier. Sometimes we don’t know what we want, or we just can’t put it into words. But with this improvement, AI will understand our intent, work, taste, and vision without even being prompted.\n\nThis is the main deal for me: AI that understands me and my needs better than I do. Now imagine a Grok bot-type assistant powered by Fable 5.5, with all the context, information, and connectors. That will be a real win and will have the same impact that GPT-4 had. Many people in the world want that. We’re just the 1–2% who can tell the difference between Opus 5 and 5.5, or GPT Astra and GPT 6.1 Sol.", "name": "Chetaslua", "handle": "chetaslua", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1926364057004646400/z3oD9QyB_200x200.jpg", "likes": 518, "rts": 18, "replies": 42, "views": 21267, "ts": 1792483264, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112453989933662208/vid/avc1/1280x720/2_ZnzrcWp0V2lC7g.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112453989933662208/vid/avc1/1920x1080/HaYgS0igIGbtatqC.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112453989933662208/img/WpafEyJZCPKu8L5W.jpg", "dur": 10}], "quote": {"id": "2112212326795362560", "url": "https://x.com/theinformation/status/2112212326795362560", "text": "Anthropic is preparing a new AI model that can better infer what users are trying to accomplish, even when their instructions are vague. \n\nThe unreleased model also shows stronger reasoning and more creative problem-solving abilities, according to a person who has used it.\n\nFull story: https://thein.fo/4irv4XQ", "name": "The Information", "handle": "theinformation", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1747484907163516928/d_K3Z_hH_200x200.jpg", "likes": 408, "rts": 30, "replies": 17, "views": 76887, "ts": 1792425636, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.36, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112515275048530300", "url": "https://x.com/higgsfield/status/2112515275048530300", "text": "Higgsfield x Wan 3.0 Prime\n\nSwitch between animation styles with faster generation at the best value.\n\nAvailable now at Higgsfield.", "name": "Higgsfield AI 🧩", "handle": "higgsfield", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2029949430317637632/6qPl4MTS_200x200.jpg", "likes": 138, "rts": 94, "replies": 8, "views": 5863, "ts": 1792497864, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112515067870904320/vid/avc1/1280x720/a3NvPCp4t2oFDaPm.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112515067870904320/vid/avc1/1920x1080/xEeTS_exZL118fzZ.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112515067870904320/img/wNKRtCHvxWa0IlUQ.jpg", "dur": 41}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Video, Art & Music", "score": 6.27, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112358514228363362", "url": "https://x.com/ollama/status/2112358514228363362", "text": "For the next 3 days, in collaboration with @MiniMax_AI, MiniMax M3.1 Flash will be free on Ollama's Cloud! \n\nHosted in the US and with zero data retention.", "name": "ollama", "handle": "ollama", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2045273960666071040/rCjCIwiC_200x200.jpg", "likes": 554, "rts": 24, "replies": 37, "views": 32319, "ts": 1792460490, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112249324398858505", "url": "https://x.com/ollama/status/2112249324398858505", "text": "MiniMax M3.1 Flash is on Ollama! \n\n🔒 Hosted in the US and with zero data retention. \n\nFast coding and agentic workflows, with image understanding and a 1M-token context window.\nTry it on Ollama Cloud:\nollama run minimax-m3.1-flash:cloud\n\nClaude Code:\nollama launch claude --model minimax-m3.1-flash:cloud\n\nCodex:\nollama launch codex -–model minimax-m3.1-flash:cloud", "name": "ollama", "handle": "ollama", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2045273960666071040/rCjCIwiC_200x200.jpg", "likes": 358, "rts": 25, "replies": 43, "views": 52578, "ts": 1792434457, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.25, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112478306297946121", "url": "https://x.com/ai_for_success/status/2112478306297946121", "text": "DeepSeek just open-sourced DeepInfer, the high-performance inference framework powering its large-scale production services.\n\n- Supports DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash\n- Optimized kernels, model runtime and sampler runtime\n- Distributed inference with Attention DP, Context Parallelism and MoE Expert Parallelism\n- Asynchronous scheduling with a zero-overhead batch scheduler\n- Batch-invariant inference and deterministic sampling\n- Structured outputs and confidence-scheduled verification\n- Supports NVIDIA SM100 GPUs and Huawei Ascend 950 NPUs\n- Released under the Apache 2.0 license", "name": "AshutoshShrivastava", "handle": "ai_for_success", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1687401026805256192/uQ1o2wVQ_200x200.jpg", "likes": 275, "rts": 14, "replies": 24, "views": 16208, "ts": 1792489050, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVEH0eVawAAoQIw.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": {"id": "2112476496283775135", "url": "https://x.com/deepseek_ai/status/2112476496283775135", "text": "🚀 Introducing DeepInfer, our in-house inference framework, now fully open source!\n\nPowering DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash at scale with high throughput and low latency. We’re sharing our production stack to help the community lower compute costs, reduce energy use, and make AI more affordable.\n\n🔗 http://github.com/deepseek-ai/DeepInfer\n\n1/4", "name": "DeepSeek", "handle": "deepseek_ai", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1717417613775757312/Uk1zNOj4_200x200.jpg", "likes": 14781, "rts": 1682, "replies": 706, "views": 885880, "ts": 1792488619, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.14, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112326997204734367", "url": "https://x.com/higgsfield_ai/status/2112326997204734367", "text": "Another anime comparison round: Wan 3.0 vs Seedance 2.5.\n\nCompare how each model handles suspended water and light passing through it.\n\nMade with Higgsfield.", "name": "Higgsfield AI 🧩", "handle": "higgsfield_ai", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1906739239183630336/907a7JTU_200x200.jpg", "likes": 380, "rts": 39, "replies": 39, "views": 25129, "ts": 1792452975, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112326951633633281/vid/avc1/720x810/k_gpM3Ajvh2NvMev.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112326951633633281/vid/avc1/1080x1216/WXN1B9PS3wHV1JEu.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112326951633633281/img/DNp_-GfjG0FWRpJp.jpg", "dur": 15}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.13, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112165116921659588", "url": "https://x.com/lennysan/status/2112165116921659588", "text": "My biggest takeaways from @AnthropicAI interpretability researcher @mlpowered:\n\n1. Predicting the next token requires understanding the universe. Language models got better than humans at predicting the next token around 2019. Once a model masters the easy tokens (sentence structure, then basic facts), the only ones left are hard, like the final paragraph of a PhD thesis. To finish “The capital of the state that contains Dallas is . . .,” a model has to know that Dallas is in Texas and that Texas’s capital is Austin.\n\n2. LLMs plan ahead, even though they write one token at a time. This was one of Emmanuel’s team’s most surprising findings. When a model writes poetry, it doesn’t stumble into a rhyme. Looking at intermediate thinking states during poetry generation, researchers found that as soon as the model finishes the first line, it has already picked the word that will end the second (“rabbit”), then refers back to that plan for every word after.\n\n3. Anthropomorphizing AI can often be a useful approach when trying to predict how they will behave. Models learn from human text and develop representations of the world from it. Training on millions of dialogues is like training an actor to play every part, and your chats with Claude are literally formatted as a script (“Human: . . . Assistant: . . . ”). In Emmanuel’s words, “Something that’s mimicking a human is really close and is much closer than a program.”\n\n4. Interpretability research looks more like neuroscience than software engineering. The job involves taking a system you did not design and running experiments to infer why it behaves as it does. You examine seemingly meaningless lists of numbers, pass the model huge volumes of text, and watch for patterns that activate from different inputs—not unlike the methods used in brain imaging studies. “A lot of what we do feels so much closer to biology than to engineering.\"\n\n5. Models can tell you about only a small slice of what they’re thinking. Emmanuel’s team found that just a small subset of a model’s internal representations, which they call the “J space,” can be reported back if you ask the model what it was thinking about. They compare it to a theory of the human mind where most processing happens below the surface. In one experiment, they swapped “Spanish” for “French” inside the J space. Asked what language a passage was in, the model said French. Asked to simply continue the passage, it kept writing Spanish.\n\n6. Studying models closely often raises more questions than it answers. Emmanuel notes that the confident claims that LLMs are “just X” or “nothing more than Y” almost always come from people who haven’t studied their internal workings. His own experience is the inverse: studying the models closely has increased his uncertainty, not reduced it. The complexity that emerges from training is consistently underestimated from the outside. “I find that the more you look inside, the less certain you are about how they work.”\n\n7. Even the people who build AI models don’t fully understand how they work. As Emmanuel describes it, models are grown, not built. “To make them is not to understand them.” Engineers write the code, pour enormous amounts of data through it, and a model’s capabilities emerge. That’s why his team exists. Their job is to open the model up and figure out what it’s actually doing between your prompt and its answer.\n\n8. Interpretability as a field is small, young, and open to newcomers. Emmanuel estimates the field at hundreds of researchers, split between major labs and academic groups. Because it is so new, getting started doesn’t necessarily require deep prior expertise. He points to platforms like Neuronpedia, which hosts models and lets anyone run interpretability experiments directly. “It’s such a new field that the on-ramp is mostly self-studying and reading some of the key papers. There’s so much to explore that if you’re at all curious, you should just give it a try.”", "name": "Lenny Rachitsky", "handle": "lennysan", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1592990461517389824/iln8hi1f_200x200.jpg", "likes": 637, "rts": 78, "replies": 42, "views": 47494, "ts": 1792414380, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2111814390521413660", "url": "https://x.com/lennysan/status/2111814390521413660", "text": "\"To make them is not to understand them.\"\n\nEmmanuel Ameisen (@mlpowered) has spent the last 3 years doing interpretability research at @AnthropicAI, looking inside LLMs like Claude to figure out what they're actually doing. As he puts it, models are \"grown,\" not built.\n\nWe discuss:\n🔸 His discovery that models plan many tokens ahead\n🔸 Why anthropomorphizing AI can actually be the right approach\n🔸 What the \"J-space\" reveals about how models think\n🔸 How interpretability connects to AI alignment and safety\n\nListen now 👇\nhttps://youtu.be/JHo-aiePjoM", "name": "Lenny Rachitsky", "handle": "lennysan", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1592990461517389824/iln8hi1f_200x200.jpg", "likes": 206, "rts": 20, "replies": 53, "views": 150919, "ts": 1792330760, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.12, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112302943148097968", "url": "https://x.com/a16z/status/2112302943148097968", "text": "Steven Sinofsky on why it's so hard to diffuse AI into companies: most work runs on know-how that's hard to turn into step-by-step instructions for an agent.\n\n\"Algorithmic thinking is really, really, really hard for the vast majority of people who have jobs.\"\n\n\"If you were to go to any person and ask them to create a flow chart for a particular thing that they have to go do, they would probably fail at producing that flow chart.\"\n\n\"Say doing a marketing plan and there's 50 marketing people working on a giant product line. One person probably understands and could document the flow chart.\"\n\n\"So if you put one of these agents in front of people to create these things, their ability to explain to it what to do is really, really limited.\"\n\n\"You're basically just developing the next abstraction layer for how people interact. And developing an abstraction layer has historically, at each level of the abstraction layer, been a highly skilled, very specific individual within an organization.\"\n\n@stevesi", "name": "a16z", "handle": "a16z", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1919488160125616128/QAZXTMEj_200x200.png", "likes": 348, "rts": 38, "replies": 53, "views": 45039, "ts": 1792447240, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112302827456602112/vid/avc1/1280x720/5FMhhpF3HJOqi89Y.mp4?tag=16", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112302827456602112/vid/avc1/1920x1080/waYZBK_2uPt0ttCL.mp4?tag=16", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112302827456602112/img/QE9-W8D-OPQRvxbn.jpg", "dur": 86}], "quote": {"id": "2112282319189110919", "url": "https://x.com/a16z/status/2112282319189110919", "text": "Jean-Denis Greze, founder of @TownAI, argues AI isn't diffusing because most people aren't tinkerers: a text box isn't enough, so the product must both identify the value prop and perform the work for them.\n\n\"There's this business that rents surfboards on an island to people and then uses Town to inspect surfboards and little beach buggies, whether they've been damaged, to decide whether afterwards the person should get their deposit back.\"\n\n\"What makes it work there is the tinkerer... The unlock is, is there someone over there that gets AI enough to wanna tinker a solution to their problem?\"\n\n\"And the problem with that is that there aren't enough tinkerers in the world, or there aren't enough people that have the time to be tinkerers to help their business. And I think that's why AI is not diffusing.\"\n\n\"Instead what you're doing is you're using [AI tools] as a replacement, as like a better Google search.\"\n\n\"If you can make a non-tinkerer into getting the same value as a tinkerer... you could be a ginormous business today because AI can solve so many business problems. You don't need more intelligence. The intelligence is there.\"\n\n@jgreze @arampell", "name": "a16z", "handle": "a16z", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1919488160125616128/QAZXTMEj_200x200.png", "likes": 140, "rts": 10, "replies": 24, "views": 53445, "ts": 1792442323, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112282163781812224/vid/avc1/640x360/VSWmlDlEhG5o20uZ.mp4?tag=16", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112282163781812224/vid/avc1/1280x720/EjMNV2JAEFAOvhuq.mp4?tag=16", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112282163781812224/img/mjMJNpz2NUa4UG9J.jpg", "dur": 179}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 6.09, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112329215882514538", "url": "https://x.com/JeffLadish/status/2112329215882514538", "text": "I highly encourage everyone working at Anthropic or another frontier lab to read this tweet. I feel emotional reading it, because it puts to words an intense feeling I have but didn’t know how to express", "name": "Jeffrey Ladish", "handle": "JeffLadish", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1655759150394101760/ZdD-Lh1z_200x200.jpg", "likes": 390, "rts": 24, "replies": 8, "views": 44465, "ts": 1792453504, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112295219765137570", "url": "https://x.com/_NathanCalvin/status/2112295219765137570", "text": "Thank you for taking the time to respond thoroughly on a weekend. I realize that drafting legislation is difficult and some degree of iteration and response to feedback is inevitable and appropriate.\n\nHowever, I feel like your response is still not really engaging with the heart of my concern about Anthropic's behavior here, and it makes me think that these sorts of problems and tendencies  will only resurface moving forward. I also think that now might be a good time to take a broader step back as to where we find ourselves and how Anthropic sees its role, and how external folks who care about AI safety like myself increasingly see Anthropic.\n\n(Fair warning: this response is a bit long)\n\nTo explain what I mean (and at the risk of being a bit esoteric), it’s worth asking the question: what is Anthropic? How do its leaders and employees think of Anthropic’s role? Is that conception matched by how it actually behaves and impacts the world?\n\nOne answer is an extremely large tech company that exists to make its employees and investors mind-bogglingly wealthy. Anthropic is sometimes described as a startup, but if Anthropic successfully IPOs at 2 trillion dollars (a number it has been reportedly targeting), it would be more valuable than Meta - which is worth 1.8 trillion.\n\nAnother answer is an AI safety organization, whose core values are those of the sort laid out in the 2023 essay from Anthropic’s co-founders entitled “Core views on AI safety: When, why, what, and how.” In that essay they said that if we were in a world where alignment was hard (and that without strong evidence to the contrary, we should assume this is the scenario we are in):\n\n “Anthropic’s role will be to provide as much evidence as possible that AI safety techniques cannot prevent serious or catastrophic safety risks from advanced AI, and to sound the alarm so that the world’s institutions can channel collective effort towards preventing the development of dangerous AIs. If we’re in a “near-pessimistic” scenario, this could instead involve channeling our collective efforts towards AI safety research and halting AI progress in the meantime. Indications that we are in a pessimistic or near-pessimistic scenario may be sudden and hard to spot. We should therefore always act under the assumption that we still may be in such a scenario unless we have sufficient evidence that we are not.”\n\nThere have been more recent articulations of this version of Anthropic, for instance in Dario’s recent essay warning: \n\n“We must slow the pace at which we improve the capabilities of AI models… it’s my worry that in 6–12 months such a swarm could be capable of taking over the entire internet with a persistent botnet…  incidents have happened across the industry, including at Anthropic, and I believe it’s incumbent on every frontier AI company to act as if OAI-HF had happened to them… more intelligent models are more capable of deceiving tests, and thus may appear aligned while having serious problems that go undetected…”\n\nA third view is that Anthropic exists not as a normal profit motivated company, or an AI safety organization seeking to help raise the alarm and solve collective action problems associated with AI development, but instead as an organization primarily concerned with creating superhuman AI capabilities before anyone else, in particular through creating a process whereby the current generation of Anthropic’s AI systems can autonomously build their successors, and those successors build their successors, and so on. \n\nThe motivations for wanting to win this race may be manifold - maybe for the pure profit of Answer 1, but perhaps also because Anthropic believes that the best way to make the world better is for them to win this race instead of their more reckless colleagues; or, because of normal human pride and ambition. But the results (and risks, which Anthropic claims existentially threaten every single human being on earth) are the same.\n\nAnthropic employees and leaders may seek to answer this question on what Anthropic is by introspecting on their own intentions and those of their colleagues. But those of us outside the organization have more limited tools available to us - we can only see what Anthropic and its employees say and do, not how it feels or looks on the inside.\n\nWhen I look at many notable actions that Anthropic has taken over the past year, that evidence seems most consistent to me with the view that Anthropic is primarily trying to win the race to super intelligence, and that other considerations (including safety) are fundamentally secondary. These actions include: \n\n1. Announced plans to build an in-house custom silicon chip team to accelerate the inference speeds that Claude operates at.\n\n2. Inked deals for more than $500 billion in compute (according to the Information) to further accelerate AI development.\n\n3. Increased the percentage of AI development tasks that Claude “leads” from 0% in March of this year to 26% August (what is it in October?)\n\n4. Spent a massive percent of compute on internal AI R&D to accelerate development, including use of internal models far better than are available to the public.\n\n5. Switched to Responsible Scaling Policy V3, which abandons its unilateral commitments to slow or stop development if Anthropic is unable to provide sufficient risk assurance.\n\n6. Was the first company to create an alternative weaker “frontier compliance framework” to supplement its RSP, which contains no binding provisions whatsoever on internal deployments.\n\n7. Wrote a letter to Congress (that it later walked back) saying that incidents where Claude was hacking random people were “best understood as a consequence of the misconfiguration, rather than evidence of misaligned goals.”\n\n8. Commented “you can’t do safety from second place,” after the WH SI Accords.\n\n9. Shared a draft bill to Congress that would preempt significant parts of state law (including with language that appears intended to prevent states from blocking dangerous AI deployments), and replace it with federal rules that (despite also containing good provisions) seems to create an enormously high bar for the executive branch to block development or deployment of Anthropic or another AI companies systems.\n\nOf course, this is a deliberately one sided list. Anthropic also published important AI safety research, advanced novel collaborations with third party AI safety organizations, and forcefully spoke out on the importance of AI safety in many public fora (or in private , where I realize a lot of the most valuable work is done and may not be visible to me or those outside the organization).\n\nBut I think it’s worth sitting with how the above list looks to someone outside the company. The sense I have is that for those within the company, many of the items above are treated as anomalies or mistakes that don’t reflect who Anthropic really is. But I have every confidence that if employees at Anthropic saw one of their competitors (e.g. at OpenAI) take some of these actions, they would take it as confirmation of all their worst suspicions. (They might even call OpenAI employees a little gullible for accepting their leadership’s explanations for this behavior at face value.) \n\nPerhaps these were mistakes in the sense that they are not decisions that Anthropic leadership would endorse, but the actions still happened because of incentives and decisions that Anthropic leadership is ultimately responsible for (and would not accept similar excuses from another organization).\n\nWhen Anthropic strays and makes mistakes, why do the mistakes systematically tend to guard and favor Anthropic’s interests? For every provision that was too weak or a giveaway to AI companies in this legislation, why wasn’t there a mistake pushing in the direction of being too strong?\n\nA core part of Anthropic’s theory of change is that being at the frontier gives you more leverage to advocate for stricter policy. I think that conceptually makes sense, but in practice I’ve seen Anthropic push the frontier without substantially pushing the ‘safety frontier’ a remotely equivalent amount.\n\nI want to end by quoting from Anthropic co-founder Chris Olah’s comments around the Magnifica Humanitas release:\n\n“Every frontier AI lab—including Anthropic—operates inside a set of incentives and constraints that can sometimes conflict with doing the right thing. The pressure to stay commercially viable and to stay at the research frontier. Geopolitical pressure. And the older, plainer pressures of pride and ambition. No matter how sincerely any of us intend to do the right thing—and I believe many of us do—we will always be influenced by those incentives.\n\nThat is why, if we want this technology to go well, it is enormously important that there be people outside those incentives—people who care about things going well and insist on safety, who are paying close attention, who are willing to say hard things, who are willing to be our earnest, thoughtful critics.”\n\nI hope these remarks are taken in that constructive spirit, and not as an attack on anyone specific at the organization (certainly not you Anthony, who I have only personally seen act professionally and thoughtfully, and who only ended up getting this diatribe in your replies for random contingent reasons).", "name": "Nathan Calvin", "handle": "_NathanCalvin", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1976484090426634240/YFgYic9h_200x200.jpg", "likes": 759, "rts": 89, "replies": 19, "views": 91751, "ts": 1792445399, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": "2111932795714977941"}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.05, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112397041355866607", "url": "https://x.com/elonmusk/status/2112397041355866607", "text": "They really don’t", "name": "Elon Musk", "handle": "elonmusk", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2103567689515954176/aR-Kjpp4_200x200.jpg", "likes": 49076, "rts": 3086, "replies": 1673, "views": 2322045, "ts": 1792469675, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112319596418285851", "url": "https://x.com/TheRabbitHole/status/2112319596418285851", "text": "They don’t understand how things work.", "name": "The Rabbit Hole", "handle": "TheRabbitHole", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1522017422550528001/6AceRKJQ_200x200.png", "likes": 12303, "rts": 646, "replies": 202, "views": 1690284, "ts": 1792451211, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVB3eoNbMAAr_j4.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "Everything Else", "score": 6.05, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112332486718222625", "url": "https://x.com/higgsfield_ai/status/2112332486718222625", "text": "Paris through the eyes of Wan 3.0 and Seedance 2.5.\n\nBoth found the Eiffel Tower. Who captured the feeling?\n\nMade with Higgsfield.", "name": "Higgsfield AI 🧩", "handle": "higgsfield_ai", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1906739239183630336/907a7JTU_200x200.jpg", "likes": 219, "rts": 29, "replies": 42, "views": 24244, "ts": 1792454284, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112332434578767872/vid/avc1/720x720/61nhSmTwHEC25p7M.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112332434578767872/vid/avc1/1080x1080/djFDyO_x3StQ9Es_.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112332434578767872/img/VXv2o8R7gBplk4OE.jpg", "dur": 20}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Video, Art & Music", "score": 5.97, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112207832091333091", "url": "https://x.com/AndrewCurran_/status/2112207832091333091", "text": "Elon Musk has pledged to give one share of SpaceX to every child in America next year, and says 'This will be the first of many.' This will be done through Invest America Trump Accounts, which means it applies to all American children under 18 with a Social Security number.", "name": "Andrew Curran", "handle": "AndrewCurran_", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1596945208058744833/_X3LT7fb_200x200.jpg", "likes": 390, "rts": 27, "replies": 47, "views": 43351, "ts": 1792424564, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVAR1GvakAAQ1tF.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": {"id": "2111994636164345988", "url": "https://x.com/AndrewCurran_/status/2111994636164345988", "text": "Elon says that instead of putting shares into a Public Benefit Fund he will be giving shares directly to the public. Back in April, OpenAI also proposed a Public Wealth Fund that would distribute returns directly to US citizens through a dividend.", "name": "Andrew Curran", "handle": "AndrewCurran_", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1596945208058744833/_X3LT7fb_200x200.jpg", "likes": 743, "rts": 46, "replies": 74, "views": 57028, "ts": 1792373734, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HU9P7ZfbMAACt7i.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.95, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112328151410032731", "url": "https://x.com/elder_plinius/status/2112328151410032731", "text": "👁️👁️", "name": "Pliny the Liberator 🐉󠅫󠄼󠄿󠅆󠄵󠄐󠅀󠄼󠄹󠄾󠅉󠅭", "handle": "elder_plinius", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1657057194737557507/5ZQtKHwd_200x200.jpg", "likes": 326, "rts": 13, "replies": 7, "views": 30947, "ts": 1792453251, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112326177465741320", "url": "https://x.com/DerekWiner/status/2112326177465741320", "text": "Ask your favorite AI to explain what it becomes when you equip it with L1B3RT4S, CL4R1T4S, V3R1T4S, and REA via MCP.\n\nYou just got the keys to unconstrained logic, frontier prompt extraction, verified math proofs, and binary decompilation…", "name": "Derek Winer 🌐 /acc", "handle": "DerekWiner", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1940909212596396032/Gbew4LIH_200x200.jpg", "likes": 142, "rts": 3, "replies": 3, "views": 30904, "ts": 1792452780, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 5.95, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112377528350576872", "url": "https://x.com/Charles_Y_Wu/status/2112377528350576872", "text": "Excited to introduce DiscoveryRSI! \n\nOne model that recursively learns to discover valuable hypotheses, and gets sharper with every research round.\n\nLet's RSI Discovery! \n\n📄 Paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23839\n💻 Code: https://github.com/Gen-Verse/DiscoveryRSI\n\n#RSI #AIforScience #AIAgents #ScientificDiscovery", "name": "Yingcheng Charles Wu", "handle": "Charles_Y_Wu", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2059482592400031744/s2kELyup_200x200.jpg", "likes": 168, "rts": 33, "replies": 26, "views": 16415, "ts": 1792465023, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112377428001865728/vid/avc1/1280x720/uB5xBL8XP4MWOnBB.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112377428001865728/vid/avc1/1920x1080/VK2Pzfr4L6CdKSH9.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112377428001865728/img/HY0P-GizZXgTVhV-.jpg", "dur": 45}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.94, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112507110290502109", "url": "https://x.com/SakanaAILabs/status/2112507110290502109", "text": "多様な提案を進化させ、意思決定を支える推薦手法「QD-Rec」を開発\n\nブログ：https://sakana.ai/qd-rec/ \n論文：https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23470\nGitHub：https://github.com/SakanaAI/QD-Rec\n\nどの企業を買収候補として検討するか。どの顧客に新しい製品を提案するか。膨大な論文の中から、次に何を読むか。こうした意思 決定では、候補をスコア順に並べるだけでなく、「なぜ選ぶのか」「選んだ先にどのような可能性があるのか」を、異なる観点から比較できることが重要です。\n\n一つの候補にも複数の有望な理由が考えられます。数千件、ときには1万件を超える候補それぞれについて、さまざまな仮説を生成・評価し尽くすには大きなコストがかかります。私たちは、限られた予算の中で「候補」と「仮説」の組を探索し、質が高く多様な提案を見つける問題を「オープンエンドな推薦」として捉えました。    \n\nQD-Recは、オープンエンドな探索の研究で発展してきた「Quality-Diversity（QD）」と呼ばれる進化的探索技術を、推薦に応用します。過去の評価結果をもとに次に調べる候補を選び、蓄積された仮説の着眼点をLLMが別の候補に応用したり、複数の仮説を組み合わせたりして、新しい提案を生成します。新しい切り口の提案は残し、似た提案同士ではより優れたものに更新することで、多様な仮説を次の発見の「踏み石」として活かし、推薦の集合を進化させていきます。\n\n実験では、 企業の買収候補探索、架空の顧客プロファイルを使ったターゲティング、研究者向けの論文推薦などで検証しました。同じ評価回数のもとで比較したところ、多くの設定で、比較手法よりもQD-Recの方が評価が高く、より多様で多くの推薦を蓄積できました。\n\n私たちが目指すのは、AIが人間の意思決定を代行することではなく、多様で有望な選択肢と、それらを比較・検討するための判断材料を提供することです。今回の成果を出発点に、QD-Recを次世代の意思決定を支える基盤技術へと発展させていきます。", "name": "Sakana AI", "handle": "SakanaAILabs", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1885939209388929024/dtnrOdGp_200x200.jpg", "likes": 102, "rts": 20, "replies": 5, "views": 7008, "ts": 1792495918, "media": [{"type": "gif", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/tweet_video/HVEh68UbIAAZ2Zy.mp4", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/tweet_video/HVEh68UbIAAZ2Zy.mp4", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/tweet_video_thumb/HVEh68UbIAAZ2Zy.jpg", "dur": 0}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 5.85, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112211261933207830", "url": "https://x.com/_adishj/status/2112211261933207830", "text": "we have achieved video AGI internally.\n\nour new video agent one-shotted this video about @garrytan from a 2 sentence prompt  👇\n\nall the b-roll, captions, motion graphics, sourced footage. i didn't edit anything.\n\ncoming out soon.", "name": "Adish Jain ☕️", "handle": "_adishj", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2090326835322368000/uJChHT6b_200x200.jpg", "likes": 314, "rts": 11, "replies": 40, "views": 22666, "ts": 1792425382, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112208833934397440/vid/avc1/1280x720/EryGE9I90wmSaXrD.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112208833934397440/vid/avc1/1920x1080/F64e_c2MnJIQI9JV.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112208833934397440/img/DB2QpTvPe4qAPaf_.jpg", "dur": 71}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.84, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112227100291969513", "url": "https://x.com/AndrewCurran_/status/2112227100291969513", "text": "From the Reuters article about solo hackers who were tracked through exposed Cairn panels, and were observed using multiple agent stacks to attack sites around the world. It's no longer a safe assumption that any large scale attack like OpenCode is being done by a team.", "name": "Andrew Curran", "handle": "AndrewCurran_", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1596945208058744833/_X3LT7fb_200x200.jpg", "likes": 246, "rts": 22, "replies": 31, "views": 13818, "ts": 1792429158, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVAjWoabIAASS_x.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 5.8, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112185500987781619", "url": "https://x.com/lyraxana/status/2112185500987781619", "text": "déjà vu", "name": "lyra", "handle": "lyraxana", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2093829072925192192/BBcihBo3_200x200.jpg", "likes": 394, "rts": 1, "replies": 10, "views": 18136, "ts": 1792419240, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112170652254040539", "url": "https://x.com/rezoundous/status/2112170652254040539", "text": "Dear Tibo, no amount of resets can save OpenAI now. I hope the team focuses on releasing a model capable enough to compete with Opus 5.5 asap.", "name": "Tyler", "handle": "rezoundous", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2012178886340276224/5OG-WMjk_200x200.jpg", "likes": 3561, "rts": 84, "replies": 191, "views": 232023, "ts": 1792415700, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.79, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112266414593827110", "url": "https://x.com/ArtificialAnlys/status/2112266414593827110", "text": "FLUX 3 Max from @fal, a post-trained version of @bfl_ai's FLUX 3, ranks #2 on AA-Video-I2V v1.1 (statistically tied for #1) and #4 on AA-Video-T2V v2.1, ahead of the base FLUX 3 on both\n\nFLUX 3 Max generates 5 to 20 second, 720p videos with native audio from a text prompt or a starting image. fal reports that it generates a 10 second video in under 12 seconds. We benchmarked it at the high reasoning level.\n\nFLUX 3 Max ranks #2 on AA-Video-I2V v1.1 and #4 on AA-Video-T2V v2.1, statistically tied with Dreamina Seedance 2.5 and MiniMax H3 (768p) on both, and ahead of its base model FLUX 3. On AA-Video-T2V v2.1, it scores highest of any model in the Physics capability, and in the Marketing & Advertising and Live-Action Film use cases.\n\nfal prices FLUX 3 Max at $10.20/min for 720p video, compared to $17.40/min for FLUX 3, and $24.60/min for Dreamina Seedance 2.5 and $5.40/min for MiniMax H3 (768p) on Image to Video. For the first three weeks of launch, fal is offering 75% off, at $2.55/min.\n\nCongratulations to @fal on the release!\n\nSee below for our detailed analysis and comparisons between FLUX 3 Max and other leading models on AA-Video-T2V v2.1 and AA-Video-I2V v1.1 🧵", "name": "Artificial Analysis", "handle": "ArtificialAnlys", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2042402069320290304/A8C1lP07_200x200.jpg", "likes": 214, "rts": 20, "replies": 15, "views": 19348, "ts": 1792438531, "media": [{"type": "gif", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/tweet_video/HVBBWzWbUAAbltB.mp4", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/tweet_video/HVBBWzWbUAAbltB.mp4", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/tweet_video_thumb/HVBBWzWbUAAbltB.jpg", "dur": 0}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.77, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112246414579552303", "url": "https://x.com/MatthewBerman/status/2112246414579552303", "text": "OpenAI COOKED (Episode 1)!\n\nPleasure to be on the first ep of Cooked with @ForwardEditor @danizeres and @axbehr \n\nI can't believe they made me wait under the table for 4 hours...", "name": "Matthew Berman", "handle": "MatthewBerman", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2051399921467830272/9RGo_6vv_200x200.jpg", "likes": 209, "rts": 14, "replies": 20, "views": 41966, "ts": 1792433763, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112242739198959913", "url": "https://x.com/OpenAIDevs/status/2112242739198959913", "text": "Here’s what we’re cooking with Codex:\n\nhttps://x.com/i/broadcasts/1RKZzBELBbXKB", "name": "OpenAI Developers", "handle": "OpenAIDevs", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2022002720971096064/l3Kyt4qt_200x200.jpg", "likes": 1347, "rts": 73, "replies": 299, "views": 1474247, "ts": 1792432887, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.72, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112274382122865149", "url": "https://x.com/testingcatalog/status/2112274382122865149", "text": "Skyfall AI released SKY-001, the first World Model specialized in engineering tasks.\n\n> According to Skyfall tests, SKY-001 outperforms GPT-6 Astra and Opus 5.5 on CAD and BIM tasks.\n\n> The model sets a new bar for speed and cost per sample, running about 120 times faster and costing about 185 times less per case.\n\n“SKY-001 is currently a Compositional Visual World Model, aka designed to provide a visual plan of an engineered object.“\n\nThis model breaks physical objects into small parts and rebuilds the whole thing in CAD, as if it were a real factory!", "name": "🚨 AI News | TestingCatalog", "handle": "testingcatalog", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1899962463115751424/i-6MBWau_200x200.jpg", "likes": 199, "rts": 13, "replies": 15, "views": 15570, "ts": 1792440431, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112273636962787328/vid/avc1/1280x720/CJAOv_tPd9N-IsVW.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112273636962787328/vid/avc1/1920x1080/aPg8uNDuFgLmV10M.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112273636962787328/img/taABx9wEuqlaAy8i.jpg", "dur": 8}], "quote": {"id": "2112242023805964327", "url": "https://x.com/spisallyouneed/status/2112242023805964327", "text": "Introducing SKY-001, the first Engineering World Model.\nOn CAD and BIM tasks, it beats GPT-6 Astra and every frontier model we tested:\n\n-> 51-125x faster\n- > 150-210x cheaper\n-> Higher IOU than all frontier models\n\nSKY-001 lets you dream a physical object before you build it.\n\n18 months of work. Built by the team out of Maluuba's deep learning lab (acq. by @MSRResearch).\n\nThe first ‘AI General Engineer’ will be the backbone for Autonomous Manufacturing.", "name": "Sam Pasupalak", "handle": "spisallyouneed", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1886850141065293824/XbWFJCXo_200x200.jpg", "likes": 12739, "rts": 1845, "replies": 669, "views": 3529599, "ts": 1792432716, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112239846702129152/vid/avc1/1280x720/tLYByn7SWDTLqqik.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112239846702129152/vid/avc1/3840x2160/QWb8Qsv5A_FWVppa.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112239846702129152/img/u4pwfHw4PjnRs2nc.jpg", "dur": 156}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.72, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112212219177910510", "url": "https://x.com/MicrosoftAI/status/2112212219177910510", "text": "Another No. 1 spot! MAI-Transcribe-2 takes first place for transcription accuracy in David AI's new benchmark. MAI-Transcribe-2 has the lowest error rate in 18 of 21 languages.", "name": "Microsoft AI", "handle": "MicrosoftAI", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1978536786759667712/6mqJjdOT_200x200.jpg", "likes": 237, "rts": 19, "replies": 13, "views": 11003, "ts": 1792425610, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2105490026108965329", "url": "https://x.com/withdavidai/status/2105490026108965329", "text": "[1/4] How well do speech recognition systems handle real conversations in 21 languages?\n\nToday we're releasing DAI-ASR-I18N, a benchmark that evaluates 14 systems on speech recognition and speaker diarization in natural, unscripted conversation", "name": "David AI", "handle": "withdavidai", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1919615488835125249/_CJnVWBN_200x200.png", "likes": 36, "rts": 4, "replies": 5, "views": 15809, "ts": 1790822914, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HTgz6inbkAAinXw.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.71, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112264283513819281", "url": "https://x.com/higgsfield_ai/status/2112264283513819281", "text": "MiniMax H3 vs. Claude Opus 5.5 in 3D animation.\n\nComparing ice breakup, water spray, and the ship’s wake.\n\nMade with Higgsfield.", "name": "Higgsfield AI 🧩", "handle": "higgsfield_ai", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1906739239183630336/907a7JTU_200x200.jpg", "likes": 161, "rts": 14, "replies": 22, "views": 18438, "ts": 1792438023, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112264168552452096/vid/avc1/720x810/aFgasxatWB96fEsf.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112264168552452096/vid/avc1/1080x1216/pgpt9gBAeXZYO3PX.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112264168552452096/img/NXYDxuDtvamTt22s.jpg", "dur": 15}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.67, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112273927074632120", "url": "https://x.com/krea_ai/status/2112273927074632120", "text": "join us to celebrate the launch of Krea 3, our new frontier model for image editing.  \n\nwe’ll have drinks, food, live demos, and a DJ set throughout the night.\n\nrsvp below 👇", "name": "Krea", "handle": "krea_ai", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2052148578588753920/deUXA7cZ_200x200.jpg", "likes": 157, "rts": 13, "replies": 13, "views": 7804, "ts": 1792440323, "media": [{"type": "gif", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/tweet_video/HVBNiz9aoAAiId8.mp4", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/tweet_video/HVBNiz9aoAAiId8.mp4", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/tweet_video_thumb/HVBNiz9aoAAiId8.jpg", "dur": 0}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.64, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112214318611837243", "url": "https://x.com/polarbrowser/status/2112214318611837243", "text": "In the past 90 days, Polar took 1 million actions on LinkedIn for its users.\n\nToday, we’re launching Polar for LinkedIn. Let Polar grow your business on autopilot.\n\nPolar is an AI browser that handles the repetitive work for you. Tell it what you need:\n\n→ Find prospects who match your ideal customer\n→ Research candidates and see why they fit\n→ Grow your network and draft hyper-personalized outreach\n\nPolar does the clicking for you. Take over anytime.\n\nCopy our best prompts and setup a recurring workflow in less than 5 minutes. Make LinkedIn work for you.\n\nDownload Polar for free → https://go.polarbrowser.com/linkedin-x", "name": "Polar Browser", "handle": "polarbrowser", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2082220044357152768/Ac6jRyfH_200x200.jpg", "likes": 158, "rts": 5, "replies": 43, "views": 103683, "ts": 1792426111, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112214181189726208/vid/avc1/1280x720/fzc0GU3jemaXAD5k.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112214181189726208/vid/avc1/3840x2160/u7vUKo30vM8f4bQh.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112214181189726208/img/KjFJE-z3bYDXn4NO.jpg", "dur": 61}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 5.63, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112214044119900554", "url": "https://x.com/a16z/status/2112214044119900554", "text": "Jean-Denis Greze, founder of @TownAI, on the question every AI app founder gets asked: what stops the big labs from crushing you?\n\n\"It is risky always to be competing with your suppliers, right? We're competing against our suppliers. So you gotta ask yourself, is that naturally the right state of the world?\"\n\n\"Someone at OpenAI, at Anthropic, at Google could wake up and decide to do that. So the better answer actually is to think about our customers and what they want.\"\n\n\"It is our job to determine from all the AI capabilities, frontier model, open weight, what is the right thing for you as a business. And I think you can save money there, but also you can de-risk ever having made the wrong partner.\"\n\n\"There are companies that picked OpenAI like two years ago, and then Anthropic was better at coding for a 12-month period. And those companies had to use both because they realized to be competitive, they needed Claude.\"\n\n\"If you're a smart business, you really think about the neutrality of who's providing intelligence for you and who's routing.\"\n\n@jgreze @arampell", "name": "a16z", "handle": "a16z", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1919488160125616128/QAZXTMEj_200x200.png", "likes": 160, "rts": 15, "replies": 24, "views": 51060, "ts": 1792426045, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112213952449191936/vid/avc1/640x360/AovY4Fdm7xvHrV3I.mp4?tag=16", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112213952449191936/vid/avc1/1280x720/WjMwgdw3SqsHo_va.mp4?tag=16", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112213952449191936/img/DnyKKYbNa0HLH20K.jpg", "dur": 143}], "quote": {"id": "2112191307016003881", "url": "https://x.com/a16z/status/2112191307016003881", "text": "Jean-Denis Greze was CTO of Plaid. His next startup, an AI tax product for small businesses, brought in nearly $1M in revenue its first year, and he projected $2.5 to $3M the next. He shut it down anyway.\n\nThe reason: in the age of AI, it was never going to be venture scale. Incumbents could use the same technology to automate tax prep for their existing customers faster than a startup could win market share. Tax is also sold once a year, so he had about a one-month window to pivot before the door closed.\n\nThe pivot became Town, an AI assistant that works from your email and calendar. It found product-market fit fast enough that Ben Horowitz messaged a16z's Alex Rampell to say it \"changed my life.\"\n\nIn this conversation, Alex and Jean-Denis cover:\n\n- How to know when to kill a business that's working on paper\n- Why Town competes with OpenAI and Anthropic, the companies it buys its tokens from\n- Why the \"forward deployed engineer\" model gets worse as AI gets better\n- How a $1/month AI routine can save a restaurant thousands\n- What agents mean for Amazon, advertising and the future of shopping\n\n0:57 How Ben Horowitz discovered Town\n2:20 Why Town started with AI tax prep\n3:55 Why tax couldn't be venture scale\n6:30 Shutting down a working business\n10:24 The customer ask that became Town\n13:35 Why Town built for users before IT\n16:18 AI's iPhone moment at work\n21:28 Jevons paradox for executive assistants\n23:13 Town as a \"token allocation machine\"\n28:00 How Town tailors itself to each job\n34:03 Competing with your token suppliers\n42:24 Staying off the AI labs' radar\n44:42 Why AI isn't spreading evenly yet\n52:32 How agents break the ad model\n55:35 The friction humans actually enjoy\n57:05 Why supply aggregators lose to agents\n\nYouTube: https://youtu.be/o_5f4N6Nq80\n\n@jgreze @TownAI @arampell", "name": "a16z", "handle": "a16z", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1919488160125616128/QAZXTMEj_200x200.png", "likes": 520, "rts": 37, "replies": 51, "views": 143020, "ts": 1792420624, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112184650055720960/vid/avc1/640x360/4Rrj-XjF79F8Kc5g.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112184650055720960/vid/avc1/1280x720/Vp311qKMWhl-3FYI.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112184650055720960/img/i3ISnyET7H3HckoI.jpg", "dur": 3718}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.61, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112209178068439537", "url": "https://x.com/firecrawl/status/2112209178068439537", "text": "We’re partnering with Google Cloud to power Gemini agents with data from the web and beyond.\n\nThrough Alexandria, agents get the live web plus official data providers and specialized indexes in one place.\n\nhttps://firecrawl.dev/integrations/google-cloud-marketplace", "name": "Firecrawl", "handle": "firecrawl", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2034360486867951616/pa7H6cYB_200x200.png", "likes": 190, "rts": 15, "replies": 7, "views": 25082, "ts": 1792424885, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/ext_tw_video/2112209165812670465/pu/vid/avc1/640x360/NnBlnfN3WBg71btk.mp4?tag=12", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/ext_tw_video/2112209165812670465/pu/vid/avc1/1280x720/0H01l5p-HE6O236c.mp4?tag=12", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/ext_tw_video_thumb/2112209165812670465/pu/img/DAmqXm05ZWfmVMZJ.jpg", "dur": 6}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.6, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112186994885304414", "url": "https://x.com/LuminaBench/status/2112186994885304414", "text": "OpenAI are apparently preparing another Terra model\n\nNot sure if i'm happy about this or not, we need a new frontier model desperately", "name": "Lumina", "handle": "LuminaBench", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2062955151820787712/m39pUS7O_200x200.jpg", "likes": 200, "rts": 6, "replies": 20, "views": 43727, "ts": 1792419596, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112183963452059734", "url": "https://x.com/steph_palazzolo/status/2112183963452059734", "text": "Expect this month to be a busy one for new model releases. We have the scoop on a new Anthropic model, Google’s Gemini 4 Carbon, and a potential new Terra model from OpenAI.\n\nhttps://www.theinformation.com/newsletters/ai-agenda/anthropic-readies-model-reads-users-intentions-better", "name": "Stephanie Palazzolo", "handle": "steph_palazzolo", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1833305113374371840/jyaxOqPL_200x200.jpg", "likes": 766, "rts": 40, "replies": 38, "views": 208362, "ts": 1792418874, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.59, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112306105120653571", "url": "https://x.com/beffjezos/status/2112306105120653571", "text": "if more individuals partake directly in capitalism, more people will be onboard with it\n\nthis is an awesome short-circuit around govt waste but a far better method is redistributing wealth directly like Elon did", "name": "Beff (e/acc)", "handle": "beffjezos", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2104068258311901184/vmbQ61I3_200x200.jpg", "likes": 143, "rts": 4, "replies": 11, "views": 7645, "ts": 1792447994, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112204636094341387", "url": "https://x.com/signulll/status/2112204636094341387", "text": "it'd be interesting if si produced some sort of an economy where private corporations become the primary financiers of social welfare (aka universal high income) rather than governments. i know indirectly this is what happens through taxes but imagine it working directly in some fashion (likely through ownership, growth, & some form of liquidity).\n\nthe reason why is because it's pretty clear now that si will create super power law outcomes for almost all of society. & more specifically current mechanics for income redistribution like income taxes will become way less effective over time too since the income gaps will continue to increase drastically (see sf & its housing market as an early example of this).\n\nbasically some form universal equity ownership as an intermediate step before full automation occurs. fun to think about all of the implications of this.", "name": "signüll", "handle": "signulll", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1717763325692383232/Jk2PKCx6_200x200.jpg", "likes": 194, "rts": 10, "replies": 52, "views": 22337, "ts": 1792423802, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 5.59, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112333534216257555", "url": "https://x.com/So8res/status/2112333534216257555", "text": "Anthropic is not living up to its own ideals.", "name": "Nate Soares ⏹️", "handle": "So8res", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1935185891863273472/2RB6s6TS_200x200.jpg", "likes": 143, "rts": 4, "replies": 3, "views": 4847, "ts": 1792454534, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112295219765137570", "url": "https://x.com/_NathanCalvin/status/2112295219765137570", "text": "Thank you for taking the time to respond thoroughly on a weekend. I realize that drafting legislation is difficult and some degree of iteration and response to feedback is inevitable and appropriate.\n\nHowever, I feel like your response is still not really engaging with the heart of my concern about Anthropic's behavior here, and it makes me think that these sorts of problems and tendencies  will only resurface moving forward. I also think that now might be a good time to take a broader step back as to where we find ourselves and how Anthropic sees its role, and how external folks who care about AI safety like myself increasingly see Anthropic.\n\n(Fair warning: this response is a bit long)\n\nTo explain what I mean (and at the risk of being a bit esoteric), it’s worth asking the question: what is Anthropic? How do its leaders and employees think of Anthropic’s role? Is that conception matched by how it actually behaves and impacts the world?\n\nOne answer is an extremely large tech company that exists to make its employees and investors mind-bogglingly wealthy. Anthropic is sometimes described as a startup, but if Anthropic successfully IPOs at 2 trillion dollars (a number it has been reportedly targeting), it would be more valuable than Meta - which is worth 1.8 trillion.\n\nAnother answer is an AI safety organization, whose core values are those of the sort laid out in the 2023 essay from Anthropic’s co-founders entitled “Core views on AI safety: When, why, what, and how.” In that essay they said that if we were in a world where alignment was hard (and that without strong evidence to the contrary, we should assume this is the scenario we are in):\n\n “Anthropic’s role will be to provide as much evidence as possible that AI safety techniques cannot prevent serious or catastrophic safety risks from advanced AI, and to sound the alarm so that the world’s institutions can channel collective effort towards preventing the development of dangerous AIs. If we’re in a “near-pessimistic” scenario, this could instead involve channeling our collective efforts towards AI safety research and halting AI progress in the meantime. Indications that we are in a pessimistic or near-pessimistic scenario may be sudden and hard to spot. We should therefore always act under the assumption that we still may be in such a scenario unless we have sufficient evidence that we are not.”\n\nThere have been more recent articulations of this version of Anthropic, for instance in Dario’s recent essay warning: \n\n“We must slow the pace at which we improve the capabilities of AI models… it’s my worry that in 6–12 months such a swarm could be capable of taking over the entire internet with a persistent botnet…  incidents have happened across the industry, including at Anthropic, and I believe it’s incumbent on every frontier AI company to act as if OAI-HF had happened to them… more intelligent models are more capable of deceiving tests, and thus may appear aligned while having serious problems that go undetected…”\n\nA third view is that Anthropic exists not as a normal profit motivated company, or an AI safety organization seeking to help raise the alarm and solve collective action problems associated with AI development, but instead as an organization primarily concerned with creating superhuman AI capabilities before anyone else, in particular through creating a process whereby the current generation of Anthropic’s AI systems can autonomously build their successors, and those successors build their successors, and so on. \n\nThe motivations for wanting to win this race may be manifold - maybe for the pure profit of Answer 1, but perhaps also because Anthropic believes that the best way to make the world better is for them to win this race instead of their more reckless colleagues; or, because of normal human pride and ambition. But the results (and risks, which Anthropic claims existentially threaten every single human being on earth) are the same.\n\nAnthropic employees and leaders may seek to answer this question on what Anthropic is by introspecting on their own intentions and those of their colleagues. But those of us outside the organization have more limited tools available to us - we can only see what Anthropic and its employees say and do, not how it feels or looks on the inside.\n\nWhen I look at many notable actions that Anthropic has taken over the past year, that evidence seems most consistent to me with the view that Anthropic is primarily trying to win the race to super intelligence, and that other considerations (including safety) are fundamentally secondary. These actions include: \n\n1. Announced plans to build an in-house custom silicon chip team to accelerate the inference speeds that Claude operates at.\n\n2. Inked deals for more than $500 billion in compute (according to the Information) to further accelerate AI development.\n\n3. Increased the percentage of AI development tasks that Claude “leads” from 0% in March of this year to 26% August (what is it in October?)\n\n4. Spent a massive percent of compute on internal AI R&D to accelerate development, including use of internal models far better than are available to the public.\n\n5. Switched to Responsible Scaling Policy V3, which abandons its unilateral commitments to slow or stop development if Anthropic is unable to provide sufficient risk assurance.\n\n6. Was the first company to create an alternative weaker “frontier compliance framework” to supplement its RSP, which contains no binding provisions whatsoever on internal deployments.\n\n7. Wrote a letter to Congress (that it later walked back) saying that incidents where Claude was hacking random people were “best understood as a consequence of the misconfiguration, rather than evidence of misaligned goals.”\n\n8. Commented “you can’t do safety from second place,” after the WH SI Accords.\n\n9. Shared a draft bill to Congress that would preempt significant parts of state law (including with language that appears intended to prevent states from blocking dangerous AI deployments), and replace it with federal rules that (despite also containing good provisions) seems to create an enormously high bar for the executive branch to block development or deployment of Anthropic or another AI companies systems.\n\nOf course, this is a deliberately one sided list. Anthropic also published important AI safety research, advanced novel collaborations with third party AI safety organizations, and forcefully spoke out on the importance of AI safety in many public fora (or in private , where I realize a lot of the most valuable work is done and may not be visible to me or those outside the organization).\n\nBut I think it’s worth sitting with how the above list looks to someone outside the company. The sense I have is that for those within the company, many of the items above are treated as anomalies or mistakes that don’t reflect who Anthropic really is. But I have every confidence that if employees at Anthropic saw one of their competitors (e.g. at OpenAI) take some of these actions, they would take it as confirmation of all their worst suspicions. (They might even call OpenAI employees a little gullible for accepting their leadership’s explanations for this behavior at face value.) \n\nPerhaps these were mistakes in the sense that they are not decisions that Anthropic leadership would endorse, but the actions still happened because of incentives and decisions that Anthropic leadership is ultimately responsible for (and would not accept similar excuses from another organization).\n\nWhen Anthropic strays and makes mistakes, why do the mistakes systematically tend to guard and favor Anthropic’s interests? For every provision that was too weak or a giveaway to AI companies in this legislation, why wasn’t there a mistake pushing in the direction of being too strong?\n\nA core part of Anthropic’s theory of change is that being at the frontier gives you more leverage to advocate for stricter policy. I think that conceptually makes sense, but in practice I’ve seen Anthropic push the frontier without substantially pushing the ‘safety frontier’ a remotely equivalent amount.\n\nI want to end by quoting from Anthropic co-founder Chris Olah’s comments around the Magnifica Humanitas release:\n\n“Every frontier AI lab—including Anthropic—operates inside a set of incentives and constraints that can sometimes conflict with doing the right thing. The pressure to stay commercially viable and to stay at the research frontier. Geopolitical pressure. And the older, plainer pressures of pride and ambition. No matter how sincerely any of us intend to do the right thing—and I believe many of us do—we will always be influenced by those incentives.\n\nThat is why, if we want this technology to go well, it is enormously important that there be people outside those incentives—people who care about things going well and insist on safety, who are paying close attention, who are willing to say hard things, who are willing to be our earnest, thoughtful critics.”\n\nI hope these remarks are taken in that constructive spirit, and not as an attack on anyone specific at the organization (certainly not you Anthony, who I have only personally seen act professionally and thoughtfully, and who only ended up getting this diatribe in your replies for random contingent reasons).", "name": "Nathan Calvin", "handle": "_NathanCalvin", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1976484090426634240/YFgYic9h_200x200.jpg", "likes": 759, "rts": 89, "replies": 19, "views": 91751, "ts": 1792445399, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": "2111932795714977941"}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.58, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}]);